    {
      "paperId": "7d05a35c15986e4d40c891ede7ca94901c762abf",
      "title": "\u2018Die Weltgeschichte ist das Weltgericht\u2019: Hegel\u2019s Early Critique of Fichte\u2019s Doctrine of Right (Jena 1801\u20101806)",
      "authors": [
        {
          "name": "Emmanuel Chaput",
          "authorId": "2074675438"
        }
      ],
      "year": 2025,
      "abstract": "In this paper, I present Hegel\u2019s critique of Fichte\u2019s political thought during the Jena period. Hegel\u2019s general critique of Fichte has often been disregarded as unconvincing and unoriginal, but I argue that when one focuses on the specifically empirical aspects of\n Hegel\u2019s critique, Fichte\u2019s doctrine of right, one can actually see not only the validity of Hegel\u2019s position, but its originality. I take it to be indicative of Hegel\u2019s future approach to philosophy, and as such, as an important step in his intellectual development.\n Focusing on the specifically empirical dimension of Hegel\u2019s critique could seem somewhat surprising since Fichte\u2019s transcendental approach should be immune to such a non-transcendental critique. But I take Hegel\u2019s point to be that insofar as Fichte commits to the ideal of\n a systematic philosophy which includes a doctrine of right, the empirical and historical should be understood as valid judicative instances for judging such a transcendental approach of right in order to avoid the accusation of formalism. In the end, I show how, according to Hegel, because\n of his transcendental presuppositions, Fichte can escape neither the accusation of formalism nor the judgment of history. This in turn points out the importance of Hegel\u2019s critique as a manifestation of the early stages of what will soon be Hegel\u2019s own philosophical position as\n presented in the Phenomenology of Spirit.",
